Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.320
TOOLBARS:
Remember toolbars?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:07.400
Back in the late 90s and early 2000s, toolbars&nbsp;
were basically synonymous with the address bar of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:13.040
Internet Explorer. You had toolbars for searching,&nbsp;
toolbars for shortcuts, toolbars for torrents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:18.240
and of course, toolbars that you accidentally&nbsp;
installed as bloatware. And some people’s browsers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:24.280
no joke looked just like this. But, that’s&nbsp;
not too surprising given that basically every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:30.560
major tech company had their own set of toolbars&nbsp;
from MSN messenger and Yahoo to AOL and ask.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:38.120
and they all seemed somewhat useful. But likely&nbsp;
the most recognizable toolbar of all time is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.680
none other than the Google Toolbar. In fact, I&nbsp;
would argue that the Google toolbar was one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.320
the main products that initially put Google&nbsp;
on the map. If you’re on the younger side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.240
you may have never seen the internet before&nbsp;
Chrome, but the truth is that Chrome was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:59.080
a rather late entry into the browser space having&nbsp;
not come out till late 2008. Up until this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:04.480
the only way to easily access Google was to either&nbsp;
install the toolbar or type in google.com into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:10.760
address bar, something that I would hope that we&nbsp;
all know is a sin. So, most people resorted to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:15.880
former. Fun fact, Sundar Pichai’s first job at&nbsp;
Google was actually to lead product management&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:22.840
for the Google Toolbar back in 2004. But since&nbsp;
then, toolbars have largely fallen out of favor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:28.200
To be honest, I can’t remember the last time&nbsp;
I saw someone with even a single toolbar. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:33.760
taking a look at Google trends, this seems to be&nbsp;
the overarching trend. Browser toolbars have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:39.240
on a downward spiral for nearly 20 years now. In&nbsp;
fact, Google themselves killed off their toolbar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:47.400
at the end of 2021, so it seems that the era&nbsp;
of browser toolbars is very much over, but why?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:52.040
It seems that having quick access to the most&nbsp;
popular services that you use right underneath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:57.360
the address bar would be rather convenient.&nbsp;
Well, it turns out the answer actually has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.280
lot more to do with maximizing control, power,&nbsp;
and influence over the web as opposed to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:15.800
sort of logical reason. So, join me, as we take&nbsp;
a look back at what really happened to toolbars.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.760
