checking out Silo in the description below. But&nbsp;
anyway, taking a look back, the story of toolbars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:20.200
dates back to the early 1990s well before the&nbsp;
internet or browsers were mainstream. Toolbars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:24.400
which were originally called ribbons, were&nbsp;
actually largely popularized by Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:30.000
who introduced them into the Office suite. These&nbsp;
ribbons were almost immediately a hit as it made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.200
the software far more useful and accessible. And&nbsp;
as you know, these ribbons are still around to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:40.600
this day and are very much a critical part of&nbsp;
any Office product. But, while Office ribbons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:46.560
took off quite early on, it took a lot longer&nbsp;
for browser toolbars to even make their debut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.520
largely because browsers weren’t really even a&nbsp;
thing yet. Of course, you had the WorldWideWeb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:57.600
browser that came out in 1990, but it wasn’t until&nbsp;
Mosaic and more notably Netscape that browsers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:04.040
really started gaining mainstream popularity. This&nbsp;
trend was simply put into overdrive in 1995 when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:09.240
Microsoft launched Interent Explorer and started&nbsp;
bundling it in with Windows. But, while browsers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.960
were really starting to gain momentum, they were&nbsp;
still quite rudimentary. Here’s how Mosaic looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:19.120
for example. It could only display static content&nbsp;
and functionality was largely limited by what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.400
browser developers were able to pack. As such,&nbsp;
it was only a matter of time until we saw the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:29.840
world of browser extensibility begin to evolve&nbsp;
starting with plug ins. This of course starts with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:37.240
the infamous Adobe Flash Player in 1996 and Java&nbsp;
in 1998. These two revolutionized the browsing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:41.880
experience as they opened the door for everything&nbsp;
from dynamic websites and video streaming to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:46.280
browser games and more intense enterprise&nbsp;
applications. And as people started getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:51.600
used to more capable browsers, they naturally&nbsp;
wanted to do more and more leading us into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:58.080
the era of browser toolbars. In the late 90s and&nbsp;
early 2000s, we saw the first 3rd party toolbars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:03.800
pop up but unlike the Office ribbons, these&nbsp;
didn’t exactly blow up in popularity. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:09.760
it was quite the opposite until a special someone&nbsp;
stepped into the field: Google. Despite eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:15.520
becoming the world’s largest internet company,&nbsp;
Google didn’t start off with grand ambitions. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:21.160
fact, it wasn’t even originally called Google, it&nbsp;
was actually called Backrub. And Backrub started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:25.120
off as an academic research project that was&nbsp;
more focused on the theoretical side of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:31.320
to organize and rank so much data. Fortunately,&nbsp;
the name Backrub didn’t stick around for long but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:36.560
Google stayed an academic project. In fact, it was&nbsp;
actually originally launched on Stanford’s network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:42.840
with the domain google.stanford.edu in 1996.&nbsp;
And it wasn’t till 2 years later that Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:48.400
was turned into a company but even this wasn’t&nbsp;
too serious. The founders were actually willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:55.280
to sell all of Google for just $1 million in 1999&nbsp;
but the buyers passed on the deal. But after this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:01.120
deal fell through, everything changed. Google was&nbsp;
no longer just a college project with a generous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:07.120
exit. It was a serious company that needed to&nbsp;
maximize users and revenue but the reality was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:13.400
that Google was competing in a rather saturated&nbsp;
space. All of the biggest companies including AOL,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:18.320
Microsoft, and Yahoo wanted to control the&nbsp;
search space. And sure, Google might’ve had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:23.760
higher quality search results but this only&nbsp;
mattered people even tried it. What Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:29.440
needed was a new way to put their search engine&nbsp;
in front of people: enter the Google toolbar.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:38.760
TOOLBARS TAKEOVER:
The Google toolbar made its debut on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:46.520
December 11, 2000 with one simple goal: make high&nbsp;
quality search results easier to access than ever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:51.200
At first, the toolbar was basically just a search&nbsp;
bar that allowed you to easily search Google from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:57.120
any website, but over the years, the toolbar&nbsp;
became much more than just that. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:01.840
after the launch of gmail, users could link the&nbsp;
toolbar with their email and load saved bookmarks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:07.040
and history. Google also introduced features such&nbsp;
as translation, autofill, and spell checking into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:12.960
the toolbar. All of these features made the Google&nbsp;
toolbar quite useful but it’s usefulness itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:18.160
wasn’t what drove it’s popularity. One of the main&nbsp;
ways that the Google made their toolbar popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:24.640
was by making it bloatware. I’m not even kidding,&nbsp;
Google partnered with a bunch of already popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:29.320
software companies and stuck deals to bundle in&nbsp;
the toolbar with their software. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:35.360
they had had such deals with RealPlayer and&nbsp;
even Sun Microsystems. Anyway, as the Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:42.040
toolbar started to gain popularity, the big boys&nbsp;
naturally stepped into the arena as well. In 2004,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:47.480
Microsoft launched an MSN toolbar, AOL launched&nbsp;
a toolbar, and Yahoo even launched a toolbar for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:53.360
Firefox. And all of these were quite popular.&nbsp;
In fact, toolbars accounted for 12% of search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:08:00.400
queries at Google, 18% at Yahoo, and a whopping&nbsp;
42% at Ask. And that’s just the search queres&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:04.320
that were directly being made through these&nbsp;
toolbars. Who knows how many people these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:09.520
toolbars actually drove to each of these services.&nbsp;
It wasn’t just these giants that were launching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:14.720
toolbars though. Every internet company you&nbsp;
could think of suddenly had a toolbar. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:20.560
one that stuck out to me the most was from Amazon&nbsp;
actually. Before Alexa became the voice of Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:27.080
Amazon actaully had a toolbar called Alexa but as&nbsp;
you would guess, not all toolbars were quite as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:33.600
benign or as helpful as Alexa which leads us&nbsp;
into likely the biggest pitfall of toolbars:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:39.080
their perception. Throughout the 2000s, toolbars&nbsp;
quickly built up an association with malware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:45.360
spyware, and bloatware. Many of toolbars that you&nbsp;
ended up with weren’t even intentional. You were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.040
just trying to download Winrar or uTorrent, and&nbsp;
all of a sudden, the next time you open up your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:55.960
browser, you’re met with this horrendous blob at&nbsp;
the top. Ironically, Google was vert much part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.960 --> 00:09:00.440
the problem but they weren’t too concerned&nbsp;
about that because they were starting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:08.120
sense a much bigger problem. You see, Google was&nbsp;
starting to become a bit too successful. In 2004,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:14.760
they would IPO and just 1 year later, they would&nbsp;
reach a market cap of $100 billion, and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:20.000
success started to make the big players rather&nbsp;
nervous. Google was no longer the scrappy startup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:24.440
with the popular search engine and toolbar. They&nbsp;
had a full slew of products including YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:30.440
Gmail, and Android, and with a market cap of&nbsp;
$100 billion, they were very much a big tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:36.480
company that was eating into the market shares&nbsp;
of Hotmail, Yahoo, and MSN. And the worst part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:41.200
about all of this was that Microsoft was that they&nbsp;
were not just standing by and watching it happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:47.000
they were literally enabling it. After all, it was&nbsp;
Internet Explorer’s generous toolbar compatibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:52.640
that had made the Google Toolbar so popular in the&nbsp;
first place. As such, it was time for Microsoft to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:59.880
put their own products first and block out leeches&nbsp;
like Google, and with that, they did absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:05.200
nothing. The reality is that while it was likely&nbsp;
that Microsoft felt somewhat threatened by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:19.494
Google’s rising dominance, they never actually did&nbsp;
anything about. Google, on the other hand, did.

00:10:19.494 --> 00:10:21.480
GOOGLE PUTS DOWN TOOLBARS:
In 2006, Sundar Pichai,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:25.560
the leader of Google toolbar development, would&nbsp;
make the bold claim that Microsoft could change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:31.880
Internet Explorer at any time to threaten Google’s&nbsp;
toolbar business. And his solution to this concern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:36.880
was for Google to create their own browser and&nbsp;
challenge Microsoft before Microsoft pulled the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:42.800
rug. Was he actually concerned about Microsoft&nbsp;
pulling the rug or did he just use this fear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:47.840
as a way to sell Chrome? To be honest, it&nbsp;
was probably more of the latter. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:53.640
Sundar had already pitched the idea of Chrome&nbsp;
before but his idea was rejected due to cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:58.840
concerns. So, it seems that Sundar cleverly&nbsp;
used the Microsoft threat to frame Chrome as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:04.800
a defensive move as opposed to an offensive&nbsp;
move, and it worked. Chrome would be green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:08.920
lit and Sundar would be placed at the helm, and 2&nbsp;
years later, we would see the launch of Chrome on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:15.080
September 2, 2008. From the very beginning,&nbsp;
the intention of chrome was pretty clear:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:21.480
cut out competitors and grow power for Google, but&nbsp;
to their credit, they did frame this really well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:25.840
Instead of saying that Chrome wouldn’t support&nbsp;
any toolbars, they pitched Chrome as a super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:31.560
clean browser that wasn’t bogged down by a bunch&nbsp;
of extensions. Now, I do want to note that Chrome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:38.520
didn’t support their own toolbar itself but this&nbsp;
isn’t really a sacrifice. After all, with Chrome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:43.760
Google had control over the entire browser.&nbsp;
Despite this clear ulterior motive though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:49.640
most people didn’t really care. They were fed&nbsp;
up with toolbars being bloatware and Internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:54.680
Explorer in general. So, when Google came out&nbsp;
with a clean browser, it didn’t take long for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.680 --> 00:12:00.040
people to switch over and for Chrome to become the&nbsp;
dominant browser. Chrome did eventually come out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:05.240
with their own store for 3rd party extensions&nbsp;
called the Chrome Webstore but they made sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:09.880
to differentiate it from toolbars such that no&nbsp;
extension would really be a threat to Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:15.880
search. And that was really the end of it. With&nbsp;
Chrome dropping support for traditional toolbars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:22.560
and Chrome becoming the dominant browser, toolbars&nbsp;
quickly became relic of the past. If anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:27.080
most users were happy to move away from&nbsp;
toolbas as they very much remembered the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:32.840
nightmares of this. So, toolbars went down as&nbsp;
just something that naturally went obsolete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:38.480
with evolving technology. But the reality&nbsp;
was that it wasn’t all that natural. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:43.600
Google that made toolbars popular in the first&nbsp;
place and it was Google that eventually killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:49.040
toolbars as well with the launch of Chrome,&nbsp;
and that’s what really happened to toolbars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:54.520
Something else that has become a relic of the past&nbsp;
is uTorrent. Check out this video to learn more.

